  "title": "Sharp modulus range for triangle counts in dense random graphs",
  "description": "<p>For fixed p in (0,1), ordinary triangles T_n counted once in G(n,p), and every positive integer sequence q_n, max over all residues a of |q_n P(T_n = a mod q_n)-1| tends to zero if and only if q_n=o(n^2). This includes q=1, bounded, composite, oscillating, arbitrarily slowly subcritical, critical and supercritical sequences. Triangle standard-deviation normalization starts at n&gt;=3; the known ordinary 2K2 supplement starts at n&gt;=4.</p><p>This is a credited complete consequence of earlier estimates. Scott's 2009 report of joint work with Tateno states the smaller all-integer modulus range, the necessary standard-deviation scale and a triangle Gaussian-cell local limit theorem. Gilmer and Kopparty's 2014 manuscript Lemmas 4-5 already imply the full sufficient range by direct modular Fourier-grid summation; the supporting deduction is assembled in the current audit and is not backdated as a printed 2014 corollary. Berkowitz supplies earlier quantitative triangle input. The main probability-mass proof imports Sah-Sawhney arXiv:2006.11369v1 Theorem 1.1; its known 2K2 obstruction, Theorem 1.3, is credited. Weber supplies a finite-window/tail/Gaussian mechanism precedent only: his independent-sum theorem and constants are not imported for dependent triangle counts. No novelty, firstness, new deep method, new local limit theorem, current worldwide openness or independent-discovery lineage is certified.</p><p>Deep Sah-Sawhney Fourier/decoupling and Gilmer-Kopparty conditioning proofs are named external imports, not fully reconstructed or audited. Mathematical transfer proofs, exact counting and moment diagnostics, scoped source-read ledgers, independently sealed AI mathematical/priority reviews and detached cross-challenges are retained. Finite tests do not prove asymptotic convergence. Floating diagnostic results have declared tolerances and are retained for normal-only scratch execution, not bitwise numerical certification. Adjacent problem30001249 overlap is not a separate discovery. Sparse/varying p and unrestricted induced, disconnected or joint-count classifications are not solved here.</p><p>The supporting archive contains proof records, manuscript TeX, fresh exact finite diagnostics, source-read scope ledgers, independent internal AI mathematical and priority reviews, and immutable historical submission records. AI tools were used extensively in research, solving and reconstruction, source review, verification, drafting, and independent internal AI adversarial review. This work is unrefereed: no external human peer review, formal proof-assistant certification, or journal acceptance is claimed.</p>",
